Oesophageal Cancer
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Progressive solid to liquid dysphagia, Unintentional weight loss, Irregular mass on endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-047
Reason for Visit: Rapid deterioration in ability to swallow even liquids, severe retrosternal pain, and violent coughing fits during attempted meals.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult offspring.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapid progression of dysphagia now encompassing liquids, accompanied by severe odynophagia and coughing indicative of a potential tracheoesophageal fistula or severe aspiration risk. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate hydration status, rule out acute aspiration pneumonia, and organize expedited admission for nutritional support and definitive imaging prior to the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't even swallow a sip of water anymore; it burns terribly and then I violently cough it all back up."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports progressive dysphagia over the past 4 months, initially localized strictly to solid foods and eventually progressing to liquids over the past 48 hours. Reports unintentional, profound weight loss of 15kg. Now experiencing severe odynophagia or painful swallowing. Notes persistent coughing and hoarseness immediately upon attempting to swallow liquids. Reports regurgitation of foul-smelling, undigested food shortly after eating. Long-standing history of heavy smoking and chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux disease.
Functional Status: Bedbound, cachectic, incredibly weak.
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete inability to maintain oral hydration or nutrition; imminent risk of severe malnutrition and dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient appears emaciated, frail, and chronically unwell. Voice is noticeably hoarse.
Physical Exam: Oral cavity is clear. Palpation of the left supraclavicular fossa reveals a firm, fixed, enlarged lymph node (Virchow's node). Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles in the right lower lobe, suspicious for early aspiration pneumonitis. Abdomen is scaphoid, soft, and non-tender. The clinical presentation explicitly demonstrates progressive solid to liquid dysphagia and unintentional weight loss; an irregular mass on endoscopy is definitively expected to confirm the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Nutritional supplement drinks left untouched on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Utilizing over-the-counter antacids without any relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Oesophageal Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Severe obstruction and suspected micro-aspiration)
Differentials:

Achalasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound cachexia, a palpable Virchow's node, and the step-wise progression from solids to liquids, whereas achalasia typically presents with equal dysphagia to solids and liquids from the onset without such rapid malignant wasting.

Benign Peptic Stricture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extremely rapid weight loss, supraclavicular lymphadenopathy, and the new onset of severe coughing during swallowing, which strongly suggest malignant invasion rather than a simple benign scar.

Oesophageal Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of typical white oral thrush plaques, and the presence of a palpable metastatic node, though candidiasis could coexist.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Requires intensive medical intervention for feeding)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with hallmark red flag symptoms of advanced upper gastrointestinal malignancy: profound weight loss, progressive solid-to-liquid dysphagia, and a palpable Virchow's node. The acute inability to swallow liquids, combined with coughing fits and localized chest crackles, indicates near-total luminal obstruction and a high probability of aspiration. This represents an acute nutritional and respiratory crisis. An urgent out-of-hours home visit was mandated to recognize the severe dehydration, diagnose the acute aspiration risk, and coordinate immediate, direct hospital admission for intravenous hydration, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and urgent endoscopic evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Facilitated direct admission to the medical ward via ambulance for IV fluid resuscitation, IV Ceftriaxone for suspected aspiration pneumonitis, and urgent gastroenterology consultation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the head of your bed elevated to at least a 45-degree angle at all times, even when sleeping. This uses gravity to prevent any pooled saliva or regurgitated stomach contents from passively spilling backward into your windpipe, directly reducing the violent coughing fits and protecting your lungs from further infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently; requires inpatient stabilization.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hospital bed with elevating headrest.
Follow-up: Inpatient management required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient begins to gasp for air, if their lips turn blue, or if they vomit a large amount of bright red blood, the family must update the 000 dispatcher immediately to upgrade the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A camera test and biopsy at the hospital are required to definitively diagnose the cause of the blockage.
Clinical Course: The immediate priority is to treat the lung infection and provide fluids through a drip. The specialist team will then determine the best way to open the food pipe or provide nutrition directly into the stomach.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must not try to drink even small sips of water. Because the tube is blocked, the water is directly entering your lungs, causing the crackles and fever we are seeing today.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A large mass has grown inside your feeding tube, completely blocking it. When you try to drink, the fluid hits the blockage and overflows into your airway, causing a chest infection. The hospital will start you on a drip to rehydrate you safely and will look at placing a small metal tube (stent) through the blockage to hold it open so you can swallow again.
Actionable Steps: While waiting for the ambulance, sit completely upright and lean slightly forward. If you feel saliva pooling in your mouth, spit it into a tissue rather than attempting to swallow it, to prevent any further fluid from entering your lungs.

